----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, R: a1-c1, R: a1-d1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-c3, P: b2-b3, P: e5-e6, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, P: f2-f3, B: f4-e3, B: f4-d2, B: f4-c1, B: f4-g3, B: f4-g5, B: f4-h6, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nLooking at the current position, White has a relatively undeveloped position but with some opportunity for quick development and central control. Black appears to have castled (king on e8, rooks on a8 and h8), and their queen is on d8. White's king has moved (KWF1), so castling is not possible. The pawn on e5 is advanced but could be supported or pushed further. The bishop on f4 is active and influences key central squares.\n\nKey observations:\n- The bishop on f4 can exert pressure along the a7-g1 diagonal and already threatens material if not challenged.\n- Pushing the e5 pawn to e6 could open lines toward the black king, especially since Black’s kingside pawns haven’t moved (f7, g7, h7 all still in place). e6 would weaken the defense of the dark squares around the black king.\n- The rook lift Rf1-e1 supports the e5 pawn and prepares potential doubling on the e-file if needed.\n- However, immediate aggression might be premature; development is still important.\n\nPromising moves:\n- **P: e5-e6** – Very strong. This undermines Black’s pawn structure, opens diagonals, and may force weaknesses near the black king. After e6, Black must respond carefully (e.g., fxe6 opens the f-file and exposes the king).\n- **B: f4-g5** – Developing with tempo, possibly pinning a knight if it were on f6, but currently no piece on f6, so less effective.\n- **R: f1-e1** – Supports e5, solid developing move.\n- **P: a2-a4** – Gaining space on the queenside, but less urgent.\n\nBest among these: **P: e5-e6** stands out as it creates immediate threats. If Black captures ...f7xe6, then Bf4g5 gains time on the queen or threatens mate ideas. Even if not captured, e6 weakens Black’s position structurally.\n\nReason:  \nPushing e5-e6 increases pressure on the center and kingside, potentially opening files and diagonals for attack. It also limits Black’s counterplay and takes space. Given that White is slightly behind in development, creating dynamic imbalances favors active play.\n\nAnswer: P: e5-e6"}
