odd), 7 (odd) → odd+odd = even → light square. So c7 is light — valid.\n\nBut what\'s on c7? Empty. However, **from f4, can the bishop reach d6? c7? b8?**\n\n- f4 → c7: yes, diagonal\n- c7 → d8 is one square up-right → so **bishop on c7 attacks d8**, where the black queen sits!\n- And the black queen on d8 is **undefended**? Let\'s see: black has pieces:\n    - pawns on 7th rank all present except d7 — which is missing → so d7 is empty → yes, d8 queen has no support from pawn.\n    - no other piece defends d8.\n\nSo **B: f4-c7** wins the black queen! That’s a massive material gain.\n\nIs this move in the valid list? Check:  \nValid moves include: `B: f4-c7` — yes! It is listed.\n\nOther moves like P: a2-a4 or developing knights are slow in comparison.\n\nAlso check if the bishop is hanging: currently, no black piece attacks f4. Black has:\n- Bishop on c8 (not developed), knight on e5? No — e5 is empty. Black knight is on e5? No — b2 has NBF1? Wait: look at row 2: "NBF1" is on e2? Wait no:\n\nRow 2: \n|PWF0|PWF0|BBF1|    |NBF1|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|\n\nSo:\n- c2: BBF1 → Black Bishop\n- e2: NBF1 → Black Knight\n\nSo black knight on e2 — can it attack f4? e2 to f4 is one forward, one right — yes, that’s a knight move. So **NBF1 on e2 attacks f4**.\n\nTherefore, the white bishop on f4 **is under threat** — if we don’t move it, black can play N: e2-f4, capturing our bishop.\n\nBut in this position, it\'s **our turn** — so we can act first.\n\nThus, **B: f4-c7** wins the black queen immediately, before black can capture anything.\n\nAfter B: f4-c7, black must respond — likely K: e8-d8 or something, but they lose the queen.\n\nThis is a decisive advantage.\n\nNo other move offers such a material gain.\n\nReason:  \nCapturing the undefended black queen with B: f4-c7 is a free winning move, exploiting the exposed position of the black queen and lack of defense on d8. Despite the risk of the bishop being captured if delayed, we act first and win material instantly.\n\nAnswer: B: f4-c7'}
